Z={
1:"Commentators note the agricultural image: a field yields what is sown, according to care and season. Jñāneśvar develops the image at length, describing the body as a field ploughed by desire and harvested by karma.",
2:"Ramana Maharshi often taught from this kind of verse. His method was to ask “who am I?” and trace the sense of “I” back to the knower that is never an object. Commentators note the phrase “in all fields”: the same knower in every body. For Advaita this is identity; for the theistic schools it is the Lord’s presence in all.",
3:"Commentators note prabhāva, “power” or “glory”, of the knower: consciousness is not passive. It lights up the whole field. Śaṅkara says the powers of the knower are its limiting adjuncts, which make it seem to act. Rāmānuja says they are the self’s real qualities of knowing and acting, which the Lord supports.",
4:"Commentators note chandobhiḥ, “in hymns”: the teaching is first sung, in the Vedas, then reasoned, in the Sūtras. The Indian tradition holds that scripture and reason should agree. Śaṅkara’s commentary on the Brahma Sūtras is one of the most influential works of Indian philosophy, and he cites the Gītā often within it.",
5:"Commentators note that avyakta, the unmanifest, is included in the field: even primal matter, the seed of the universe, is known and therefore not the knower. Modern readers sometimes compare this to the difference between the brain, which can be studied, and awareness itself, which does the studying. The Gītā places everything that can be observed in the field.",
6:"Commentators note dhṛti, steadfastness, listed as part of the field: even our strengths are modifications of nature. Śaṅkara says the knower is beyond both strengths and weaknesses. This can be freeing: one need not be proud of one’s steadiness or ashamed of one’s weaknesses; both are part of the field, to be cared for and used.",
7:"Commentators note that kṣānti, patience or forbearance, and ārjava, uprightness, are central virtues in many Indian lists. Śaṅkara’s Vivekacūḍāmaṇi names forbearance among the six qualities of the seeker. The order matters: humility first, because a proud mind cannot learn. Many teachers say this list is a better test of spiritual progress than any experience.",
8:"Commentators note that the verse includes anahaṅkāra, absence of ego, alongside seeing suffering. The two are linked: seeing that ageing, illness and death come to all removes the ego’s illusion of being special. The Buddha made the same observation the starting point of his teaching, and the Gītā here makes it part of knowledge.",
9:"Commentators note the phrase nityaṃ ca sama-cittatvam, “constant evenness of mind”, in desired and undesired events. Family life brings both: births and deaths, success and failure, joy and grief. The verse asks for steady love through all of it. Jñāneśvar compares such a person to a lotus in water, rooted but untouched.",
10:"Commentators note vivikta-deśa-sevitva, “resorting to solitary places”. Many great teachers withdrew for periods: the Buddha under the Bodhi tree, Śaṅkara in the Himalayas, Ramana on Arunachala. Even busy householders can find short times of solitude, early morning or late evening. Some teachers say the inner solitude matters more than the outer.",
11:"Commentators note tattva-jñānārtha-darśanam, “keeping in view the goal of true knowledge”. Knowledge here is not collected for its own sake but for freedom. Śaṅkara says one who sees the goal is motivated to practise all the other qualities. Without the goal in view, even good qualities can become a source of pride.",
12:"Commentators note the promise amṛtam aśnute, “one tastes immortality”. Knowing Brahman is described as tasting, not just understanding: an experience. The Kena Upaniṣad (2.3) says Brahman is known by those who say it is not known, and not known by those who claim to know it. Humility is the doorway.",
13:"Commentators note that Brahman “dwells enveloping all”, sarvam āvṛtya tiṣṭhati. Rāmānuja’s school uses this to describe the Lord’s pervasion of all beings as their inner self. Śaṅkara’s school uses it to describe the one consciousness behind all experience. Both lead to reverence for every living being as a place of the Divine.",
14:"Commentators note asaktam sarva-bhṛt, “unattached, yet supporting all”: the ideal of detached care that the Gītā asks of us is first found in the Divine itself. Śaṅkara says the Self supports all experience without being touched by it. Many find this a model for parenting, teaching and leadership: sustain fully, cling not at all.",
15:"Commentators note sūkṣmatvāt avijñeyam, “unknowable because subtle”: Brahman is not hidden by distance but by subtlety. It is too close and too fine to be seen as an object. Some teachers compare it to the eye, which sees everything except itself. The verse turns the search inward.",
16:"Commentators note grasiṣṇu prabhaviṣṇu ca, “devourer and creator”: the one reality both brings forth and takes back. Śaṅkara says this shows Brahman as the cause of origin, sustenance and dissolution, the definition given in Brahma Sūtra 1.1.2. Rāmānuja says these are the Lord’s three cosmic functions.",
17:"Commentators note tamasaḥ param, “beyond darkness”, a phrase from the Śvetāśvatara Upaniṣad (3.8) that also appeared in 8.9. The light within is called jñāna-gamya, “reached by knowledge”. Śaṅkara says it is reached by the qualities of 13.7–11. Rāmānuja says it is reached by devotion and knowledge together.",
18:"Commentators note mad-bhāvāya upapadyate, “becomes fit for my state”. Śaṅkara reads it as oneness with the Lord. Rāmānuja reads it as sharing his nature. Jñāneśvar describes it as the devotee becoming like the one he loves, as iron in fire glows like fire.",
19:"Commentators note that Indian philosophy argued for centuries over whether matter and spirit are two, one, or related as body and soul. The Gītā uses Sāṅkhya vocabulary but adds the Lord above both. Śaṅkara, Rāmānuja and Madhva each built their systems partly on this verse.",
20:"Commentators note that pleasure and pain are said to be experienced by puruṣa, spirit, not by prakṛti. Matter does not feel; consciousness feels. Śaṅkara says the experience is due to identification with the body. Rāmānuja says the self truly experiences, through the body, according to its karma. Either way, the one who feels can learn to see how feeling arises.",
21:"Commentators note sad-asad-yoni, “good and evil wombs”: the quality of one’s birth follows the quality of one’s attachments. Śaṅkara says attachment to sattva leads to good births, to rajas and tamas to lower ones. The idea becomes fully developed in chapter 14. Its practical teaching is simple: what we cling to shapes where we go.",
22:"Commentators note anumantā, “the permitter”: the inner witness allows actions to happen without forcing them. Śaṅkara says the Self, by its mere presence, permits the body and mind to act. Rāmānuja says the Lord permits each being’s actions according to their karma and freedom. Many find comfort in the idea of a witness who sees everything and judges gently.",
23:"Commentators note that this knowledge frees one from rebirth, na sa bhūyo’bhijāyate. Śaṅkara says knowledge burns the seeds of future births. Rāmānuja says the self who knows its distinction from matter is not born again. The verse places knowledge above any particular way of life.",
24:"Commentators note that the three paths are named for seeing the Self, paśyanti: the goal is direct vision, not only belief. Śaṅkara says meditation is the direct means, knowledge and action the preparation. Rāmānuja says karma yoga leads to self-vision for most people. The Gītā honours every route that ends in seeing.",
25:"Commentators note śruti-parāyaṇāḥ, devoted to what they hear: such people may never read scripture, but they listen and follow. Sant Kabīr, who could not read or write, became one of India’s great spiritual poets by listening and practising. The verse honours the wisdom of the unlettered devotee.",
26:"Commentators note that every being, moving and unmoving, from plants to people, is a union of field and knower. Jain philosophy also holds that plants and even elements have souls. The Gītā’s view gives every living thing dignity. Śaṅkara says the union is like a rope mistaken for a snake; Rāmānuja says it is real but temporary.",
27:"Commentators note vinaśyatsv avinaśyantam, “the imperishable among the perishing”. Doctors, nurses and caregivers who sit with the dying sometimes say they sense something that does not die. The verse invites seeing that in every face, especially when bodies fail.",
28:"Commentators note that this verse gives an ethical reason for non-harm grounded in vision rather than rules. If the same Lord dwells in all, harm to another is harm to the Lord and to oneself. Śaṅkara says the one who sees thus reaches the highest goal. The idea strongly influenced Indian ethics of compassion.",
29:"Commentators note the double vision: seeing nature as the doer, and the Self as not the doer. Śaṅkara says this frees one from merit and sin. Rāmānuja says it frees one from pride. In daily life, many find it useful to see habits and moods as nature at work, which makes them easier to change without self-blame.",
30:"Commentators note vistāram, “spreading out”: the many come from the One, like sparks from a fire (Muṇḍaka 2.1.1). Śaṅkara says seeing this is becoming Brahman. Rāmānuja says it is reaching Brahman, the Lord of all. The vision of unity in diversity is one of the deepest themes of Indian thought.",
31:"Commentators note śarīra-stho’pi, “though dwelling in the body”: freedom is not after death only, but now. Śaṅkara says the Self in the body is already free; ignorance hides it. Rāmānuja says the self is pure by nature, though bound by karma. The verse gives hope to anyone who feels stained by the past.",
32:"Commentators note that space is called subtle, saukṣmya, and therefore untouched. The subtler something is, the less it is affected by gross things. Śaṅkara says the Self is subtler than space, so even less affected. Many meditation teachers use the image of the sky, through which clouds pass without staining it.",
33:"Commentators note kṣetrī, “the owner of the field”: the knower is like the sun to the body. Śaṅkara says one Self lights all bodies. Rāmānuja says each self lights its own body. Both agree the knower is the source of awareness in the body, not its product.",
34:"Commentators note bhūta-prakṛti-mokṣa, “freedom from the nature of beings”: this knowledge leads not only to understanding but to liberation. Śaṅkara says it is freedom from ignorance. Rāmānuja says it is freedom from the bondage of matter. The chapter ends where it began: with the field and its knower, now clearly distinguished.",
}
for _k,_t in Z.items():
    _a=DEEP.get(_k,('','',''))
    DEEP[_k]=(_a[0],(_a[1]+' '+_t).strip(),_a[2])
